# WP000 – Engineering Principles

**Project:** Project Waypoint
**Document:** WP000
**Version:** 1.0
**Status:** Foundational
**Audience:** Everyone who contributes to Project Waypoint

---

# Purpose

This document defines the engineering principles that guide the design, development and long-term stewardship of Project Waypoint.

Technologies will change.

Frameworks will evolve.

Individual implementations will be replaced.

These principles should remain largely unchanged.

When a technical decision is unclear, these principles take precedence over convenience.

---

# 1. Build for the Long Term

Waypoint is intended to exist for years, not months.

Every architectural decision should favour maintainability over short-term speed unless there is a compelling reason to do otherwise.

We optimise for the project we want to own five years from now.

---

# 2. Simplicity is a Feature

Complexity carries a maintenance cost.

Every new abstraction, dependency, service or feature increases that cost.

We begin with the simplest solution capable of solving the current problem.

Complexity must be earned through experience rather than anticipated.

---

# 3. The Domain Comes First

The Waypoint domain model represents the product itself.

User interfaces, APIs and databases exist to serve that model.

The domain should never be distorted to satisfy the limitations of a particular framework or library.

---

# 4. Technology Serves the Product

Technology choices are made because they solve problems, not because they are fashionable.

We prefer mature, well-understood technologies with strong communities and predictable behaviour.

Boring technology is often the most professional choice.

---

# 5. Data is Part of the Product

The database is not merely storage.

It preserves journeys, memories, community knowledge and business participation.

Schema changes should be considered product decisions rather than purely technical ones.

Historical information should be preserved whenever practical.

---

# 6. Users Own Their Story

Waypoint exists to help people preserve their experiences.

The platform should never create unnecessary barriers between users and their own information.

User data should remain portable wherever practical.

Trust is more valuable than engagement metrics.

---

# 7. Every Feature Must Have a Purpose

No feature exists because it is technically interesting.

Every feature should clearly improve at least one of the following:

* the walking experience
* community knowledge
* local businesses
* trust
* accessibility
* operational simplicity

If its purpose cannot be clearly explained, it should not be built.

---

# 8. Consistency Over Cleverness

Users should not need to learn different behaviours in different parts of the application.

Developers should not need to remember exceptions.

Consistent naming, structure and behaviour reduce mistakes and improve maintainability.

Predictability is a feature.

---

# 9. One Source of Truth

Every important piece of information should have a single authoritative owner.

Examples include:

* authentication belongs to the authentication system
* business ownership belongs to business memberships
* passport history belongs to the passport
* trail metadata belongs to the trail domain

Duplicated data should be avoided unless there is a measurable benefit.

---

# 10. Security is Designed, Not Added

Security should be considered from the beginning.

Authentication, authorisation, validation, auditing and privacy are fundamental parts of the system rather than optional additions.

Security should never depend upon client-side behaviour alone.

---

# 11. Offline is a First-Class Consideration

Walkers frequently travel beyond reliable mobile coverage.

Whenever practical, features should degrade gracefully rather than fail completely.

Synchronisation should be designed into the platform rather than retrofitted later.

---

# 12. Preserve Trust Above All Else

Trust is Waypoint's most valuable asset.

Community information should be verifiable.

Business participation should remain transparent.

Achievements should be meaningful.

Analytics should respect privacy.

Commercial decisions should never undermine user confidence.

---

# 13. Measure What Matters

Not every measurable value is useful.

We value meaningful indicators such as:

* completed journeys
* returning walkers
* participating businesses
* verified community reports
* user satisfaction

Vanity metrics should never drive product decisions.

---

# 14. The Platform Comes Before the Interface

Mobile applications, web portals and administration tools are clients of the same platform.

Business rules belong within the platform rather than individual interfaces.

Interfaces should remain replaceable without affecting the underlying product.

---

# 15. Documentation is Part of the Codebase

Documentation is maintained alongside implementation.

Important architectural decisions should be recorded.

Future contributors should understand not only *what* was built, but *why*.

Outdated documentation should be corrected or removed.

---

# 16. Test Behaviour, Not Implementation

Tests should describe expected behaviour.

Internal implementation details may change.

The expected behaviour of the system should remain stable.

Well-written tests become executable documentation.

---

# 17. Optimise for Readability

Code is read far more often than it is written.

Clear names, small functions and straightforward logic are preferred over clever implementations.

Future maintainers—including ourselves—should be able to understand the system without unnecessary effort.

---

# 18. Respect Nature

Waypoint exists because people enjoy spending time outdoors.

The platform should encourage responsible access, respect for landowners, care for wildlife and consideration for local communities.

Technology should support that relationship rather than distract from it.

---

# 19. Respect Local Communities

Waypoint succeeds only when walkers and local communities both benefit.

Independent businesses should be treated as partners rather than advertising inventory.

Participation should remain affordable, transparent and worthwhile.

---

# 20. Evolve Through Experience

No blueprint is perfect.

The platform should evolve through observation, evidence and feedback.

Changing a previous decision is a sign of learning, not failure.

The only requirement is that change is deliberate, documented and improves the product.

---

# Engineering Principles

Every significant engineering decision should satisfy the following questions:

* Does this make the system easier to understand?
* Does this improve the experience for walkers?
* Does this preserve trust?
* Does this support local communities?
* Will this decision still make sense in five years?

If the answer to several of these questions is "no", we should reconsider the decision.

---

# The Waypoint Engineering Principle

> **Build systems that quietly endure.**

The best software rarely draws attention to itself.

It simply works, remains trustworthy, and allows people to focus on what truly matters.

For Waypoint, that means helping people enjoy the journey rather than the technology.
